WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:04.127
- [Announcer] Thank you to
Policygenius for supporting PBS.

00:00:04.127 --> 00:00:09.000
"I just love paying my
taxes," said no American ever.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.840
At best, it's a stressful
mess of paperwork,

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:14.630
and at worst, a full blown
theft by the government

00:00:14.630 --> 00:00:16.240
of your hard earned dollars.

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:19.470
- Hating taxes seems to
be in our cultural DNA,

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:22.530
and we have a history of
celebrating those who defy them

00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:25.310
from Robin Hood to the Boston Tea Party

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:26.860
to a certain former President

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:30.460
who bragged about being smart
for not paying his fair share.

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:32.810
- Although, you could say
that robbing from the rich

00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:35.050
to feed the poor is itself a form

00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:37.410
of aggressively progressive taxation.

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:41.350
- And yet, taxes are an integral
part of human civilization.

00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:44.280
For 5,000 years, they've
been the necessary price

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:45.740
of a functioning society,

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:48.330
and if you itemized all the things you get

00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:50.380
in exchange for your tax dollars,

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:52.660
the vast majority of
people would probably opt

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:55.570
to take that deal than go
without those benefits.

00:00:55.570 --> 00:00:57.940
That's not to say there
haven't been unfair

00:00:57.940 --> 00:00:59.830
or oppressive tax systems,

00:00:59.830 --> 00:01:02.100
but at this point, we should
admit that Ben Franklin

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:03.850
was right when he said that taxation

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:05.970
is inescapable as death.

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:07.560
So why can't we accept that?

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:09.260
Why all the hate?

00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:11.843
(bright music)

00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:19.910
- Let's try a thought experiment.

00:01:19.910 --> 00:01:24.110
Say your town decides to revamp
its public parking system.

00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:25.660
They remove all the parking meters

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:27.500
and replace them with sensors

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:29.650
that can identify the owner of a car.

00:01:29.650 --> 00:01:31.750
They let everyone park wherever they want

00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:32.680
whenever they want,

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:34.400
and then, at the end of the year,

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:36.450
you get a bill for all
the parking you did.

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:39.270
- Even though this sounds
easier than the previous system,

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:40.350
there's a good chance,

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:42.350
according to some behavioral economists,

00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:45.340
that it would start to
make people very angry.

00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:47.380
That's because they'd grow accustomed

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:48.830
to parking being free

00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:50.720
and start to perceive the year end bill

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:52.800
as an intrusive penalty.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:55.430
When you disconnect the
cost from the benefit,

00:01:55.430 --> 00:01:57.230
the benefit gets taken for granted

00:01:57.230 --> 00:02:00.220
and the cost can start
feeling more like a loss,

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:02.650
and humans hate loss.

00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:04.990
- It's called loss aversion,

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:09.210
and it's a big part of
why we hate paying taxes.

00:02:09.210 --> 00:02:10.970
Every time you get a paycheck,

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:14.330
your pay stub reminds you
how much of it you lost

00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:16.770
before it even got into your hands.

00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:20.260
A bimonthly sting of primal anxiety.

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:22.150
Someone's taking my stuff!

00:02:22.150 --> 00:02:25.340
And yet, there's very
little benefit awareness

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:27.070
to balance that out.

00:02:27.070 --> 00:02:30.300
How often do you drive
down the highway thinking,

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:32.730
look at my tax dollars at work?

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:34.230
When you call 911,

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:36.830
you don't credit the IRS for their role

00:02:36.830 --> 00:02:38.940
in getting a firetruck to your door.

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:40.330
When we sit down to dinner,

00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:42.100
we don't thank the FDA

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:44.570
for making sure there's no poison in it.

00:02:44.570 --> 00:02:47.040
In a 2008 poll by Cornell,

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:51.250
57% of respondents said that
they had never participated

00:02:51.250 --> 00:02:55.470
in a social program, even
though, almost all of them had.

00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:59.660
Taxes pay for so many things
that we rely on everyday.

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:03.450
There's no way we could
mentally keep track of them all,

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:05.930
and as a result, taxes can often feel

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:10.000
less like a transaction and
more like getting ripped off.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:13.000
- Loss aversion doesn't
just mean we hate losses,

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:16.110
but that we hate them
more than we like gains.

00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:18.040
This is very true at tax time.

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:20.770
A study found that people
who owed money to the IRS

00:03:20.770 --> 00:03:22.870
were more likely to cheat on their taxes

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:25.120
than those who were due for a rebate.

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:27.610
In other words, we're more
likely to break the law

00:03:27.610 --> 00:03:30.100
to save a buck than to make a buck.

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:33.100
The difference between how
much Americans owe in taxes

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:36.890
and how much they voluntarily
pay is known as the tax gap,

00:03:36.890 --> 00:03:40.660
and it's estimated to be
around $406 billion a year.

00:03:40.660 --> 00:03:43.010
The majority of tax evasion
is committed by people

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:45.630
who pay their taxes at
the end of the year,

00:03:45.630 --> 00:03:49.230
like investors, business owners
and independent contractors

00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:51.240
rather than those who have had it withheld

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:52.450
from their paychecks.

00:03:52.450 --> 00:03:56.580
- Our national hatred of
taxes does have an upside.

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:59.080
The government can use the tax code

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:02.630
to subtly encourage
positive social behavior,

00:04:02.630 --> 00:04:05.120
commonly called nudges.

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:08.600
Charitable deductions nudge
people to be generous.

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:12.180
Mortgage deductions nudge
people to be homeowners.

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:14.190
Conversely, tax penalties

00:04:14.190 --> 00:04:16.790
can discourage undesirable behavior

00:04:16.790 --> 00:04:19.060
like smoking or pollution,

00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:22.180
but this unfortunately can reinforce

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:25.790
the perception of taxes as a punishment

00:04:25.790 --> 00:04:29.820
rather than a transaction
or a social obligation.

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:31.400
Why should I be punished?

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:33.560
The thinking goes for doing exactly

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:38.190
what our capitalist system
encourages us to do, make money.

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:41.310
- These nudges called tax expenditures

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:43.260
have been shown to be effective

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:45.410
at influencing human behavior,

00:04:45.410 --> 00:04:47.540
but as well meaning as they might be,

00:04:47.540 --> 00:04:51.090
they also tend to benefit
the wealthy and corporations

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:54.170
because they're the ones who
have the time and resources

00:04:54.170 --> 00:04:56.170
to investigate all the little tricks

00:04:56.170 --> 00:04:57.740
that can save you a buck.

00:04:57.740 --> 00:05:01.420
They also make the U.S.
tax code complicated,

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:03.080
very complicated.

00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:04.280
- According to the IRS,

00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:07.810
it takes the average
American about 11 hours

00:05:07.810 --> 00:05:09.420
to file their taxes.

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:12.260
It's hard to convey to
our international viewers

00:05:12.260 --> 00:05:14.830
how difficult and confusing filling out

00:05:14.830 --> 00:05:16.370
all those forms can be

00:05:16.370 --> 00:05:18.760
while knowing that any mistake you make

00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:21.370
can land you in hot water with the Feds.

00:05:21.370 --> 00:05:25.510
So it's not surprising that
many people, myself included,

00:05:25.510 --> 00:05:28.130
opt to pay someone else to do it.

00:05:28.130 --> 00:05:31.500
Americans spend roughly $10 billion a year

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:33.340
on professional tax preparers

00:05:33.340 --> 00:05:37.830
and another two billion on
tax preparation software.

00:05:37.830 --> 00:05:39.540
In time and money,

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:43.330
it's estimated that the tax
filing system costs the U.S.

00:05:43.330 --> 00:05:48.330
between 140 and $215 billion a year.

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:52.590
- And the weird thing is it
doesn't have to be this way.

00:05:52.590 --> 00:05:56.430
For most Americans, everything
you send to the IRS,

00:05:56.430 --> 00:05:58.000
they already about you.

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:00.050
They receive all the same forms

00:06:00.050 --> 00:06:02.350
that your employer and bank send to you,

00:06:02.350 --> 00:06:04.550
and as long as you're
not itemizing deductions,

00:06:04.550 --> 00:06:06.900
they don't technically
need you to do the homework

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:09.540
like some kind of sadistic math teacher

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:12.170
who will send you to
prison for a wrong answer.

00:06:12.170 --> 00:06:14.730
- The U.S. is one of the
only wealthy countries

00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:18.260
that puts its citizens through
this annual stress test.

00:06:18.260 --> 00:06:21.280
In countries like the
Netherlands, Sweden, Japan,

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:23.210
the process only takes minutes.

00:06:23.210 --> 00:06:24.980
The government sends you a card

00:06:24.980 --> 00:06:27.110
with all the calculations already done.

00:06:27.110 --> 00:06:28.580
You can check the math if you want,

00:06:28.580 --> 00:06:30.980
otherwise, just sign it and send it back.

00:06:30.980 --> 00:06:31.813
- That's it?

00:06:31.813 --> 00:06:32.646
- That's it.

00:06:32.646 --> 00:06:33.479
- Well, who wouldn't want that?

00:06:33.479 --> 00:06:35.760
- Well, the tax preparers, for one.

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:36.950
As we mentioned before,

00:06:36.950 --> 00:06:39.340
it's a billion dollar industry

00:06:39.340 --> 00:06:42.370
and they rely on taxes
to be such a headache

00:06:42.370 --> 00:06:44.750
that no one would want
to do it themselves.

00:06:44.750 --> 00:06:48.930
They've spent a lot of money
lobbying against legislation

00:06:48.930 --> 00:06:51.520
that would simplify the filing process,

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:55.260
and they even persuaded
the IRS not to offer

00:06:55.260 --> 00:06:57.690
its own free filing service.

00:06:57.690 --> 00:06:59.950
- Another group that
prefers things this way

00:06:59.950 --> 00:07:02.390
is anti-tax activists.

00:07:02.390 --> 00:07:05.970
If your mission is to persuade
people that taxes are bad,

00:07:05.970 --> 00:07:08.880
it helps if they associate
them with negative emotions

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:10.820
like frustration and fear.

00:07:10.820 --> 00:07:14.600
As Ronald Reagan famously
put it, "Taxes should hurt."

00:07:14.600 --> 00:07:16.590
The last thing they want is for Americans

00:07:16.590 --> 00:07:19.700
to have a more pleasant
relationship with the IRS.

00:07:19.700 --> 00:07:21.050
- And we should.

00:07:21.050 --> 00:07:23.200
Aside from the headache of filing,

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:25.000
we don't have it so bad,

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:27.270
at least compared to other countries.

00:07:27.270 --> 00:07:29.390
According to the Tax Policy Center,

00:07:29.390 --> 00:07:34.390
U.S. tax revenue equaled 24%
of gross domestic product.

00:07:34.630 --> 00:07:38.050
Well below the 34% weighted average

00:07:38.050 --> 00:07:40.440
for other OECD countries.

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:45.440
Only Chile, Ireland and Mexico
were lower on that list.

00:07:45.660 --> 00:07:49.200
Americans seem to be in
the paradoxical situation

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:52.000
of having some of the
lowest taxes in the world,

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:54.430
but the most angst about them.

00:07:54.430 --> 00:07:55.940
- But there is a bright side.

00:07:55.940 --> 00:07:57.310
Despite our hangups,

00:07:57.310 --> 00:08:00.910
Americans are actually really
good about paying their taxes.

00:08:00.910 --> 00:08:04.070
Our voluntary compliance
rate, a fancy way of saying

00:08:04.070 --> 00:08:07.560
how many people pay their
taxes honestly and on time

00:08:07.560 --> 00:08:10.700
is higher than most
industrialized nations.

00:08:10.700 --> 00:08:13.470
Surveys find that the
vast majority of Americans

00:08:13.470 --> 00:08:16.220
across the political
spectrum see tax paying

00:08:16.220 --> 00:08:18.470
as a patriotic civic duty.

00:08:18.470 --> 00:08:21.620
In fact, the only thing that
seems to shake our faith

00:08:21.620 --> 00:08:23.670
in the system is the suspicion

00:08:23.670 --> 00:08:26.610
that others aren't
paying their fair share.

00:08:26.610 --> 00:08:28.700
Why should I sweat over all these forms

00:08:28.700 --> 00:08:31.380
when rich people and
corporations are cheating?

00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:34.240
- That's why many behavioral
economists believe

00:08:34.240 --> 00:08:35.810
that the two best ways

00:08:35.810 --> 00:08:38.960
to improve America's contentious
relationship with taxes

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:42.760
would be making it easier
for ordinary people to file

00:08:42.760 --> 00:08:44.480
and closing the loopholes

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:47.260
that make the game feel
rigged against them.

00:08:47.260 --> 00:08:50.300
- After all, the IRS is
another government agency

00:08:50.300 --> 00:08:52.320
that's funded by your taxes.

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:55.290
They serve you by keeping
the nation's lights on,

00:08:55.290 --> 00:08:56.200
so to speak.

00:08:56.200 --> 00:08:58.550
We don't have to think
of them as the Boogeyman,

00:08:58.550 --> 00:09:03.550
but as the friendly
neighborhood paycheck shrinker.

00:09:03.811 --> 00:09:05.671
- Maybe that's too optimistic.

00:09:05.671 --> 00:09:06.856
- Yeah.

